hey guys how's it going my name is Danny  and today I want to talk about these  concrete things also known as Brutalism  [Music]  like why would someone intentionally  design something so bland and imposing  people with absolutely no interest in  architecture have expressed a lot of  passion and hate for it  but if you talk to any architect or  architectural enthusiast and ask them  about Brutalism they're gonna say oh my  god Brutalism is so like you delight the  texture great poetry how can something  so beloved by one group of people also  be so hated by another Brutalism is very  interesting because it's not just about  the aesthetic of the style that made  them so popular but also the intent and  the morals behind it Brutalism became  popular in the 60s to the end of the 70s  but the mass use of concrete that  started much before that in the 1920s at  that time concrete was popping up  everywhere buildings bridges roads and  it was cheap you get into massive  quantities and it could span huge  distances and you could form them into  any shape that you wanted in fact  concrete is the second most consumed  product in the world after water so  after the Second World War a lot of  countries were torn apart and the  governments were strategizing on how to  rebuild these broken states while also  creating a perception of hope and social  change in the people and so they hired  architects and they funneled a lot of  money into creating this new vision the  governments entrusted architects with  complete freedom to test out their ideas  these architects they had a very strong  idea about what a socialist society  should look like and they couldn't  believe they were creating  spaces for the people in their buildings  they wanted complete honesty and  transparency and they wanted to show  exactly how they were built and what  they were made out of and what their  functions were they just left the  concrete raw they were against any sort  of symbols or ornamentation that were  pretty common in the styles that came  before them the manual labor to do these  delicate details were super expensive so  they believed that by getting rid of  them and highlighting only the  functional elements of the building they  believed that they were creating a new  Democratic building type what's  interesting is that to achieve this raw  natural look the sneaky architects were  actually going into the concrete and  detailing it to make it look more rough  which added to the manual labor so a lot  of these buildings focus on the public  spaces with massive atriums and  balconies that would spill out onto  walkways they had open stairwells and  corridors that people could just roam  around and they had lots of water  features and a lot of planters the one  little detail that they didn't realize  was that people don't really actually  like hanging out in these massive  concrete spaces so these faces became  neglected and it started attracting  unwanted activities concrete itself  deteriorates and weathers pretty badly  and the lack of maintenance kind of took  a toll and made them look really dirty  and terrible but aside from these little  failures I think these heroic socialist  ideals behind Brutalism is what really  gets architects going  and I think it's more interesting these  days because a lot of architects they  built for developers or capitalist  society and even when we're working for  the government the majority of us rarely  get that kind of creative freedom unless  you're Frank Gehry or yakking old and I  think the second reason is the quality  of the concrete itself it's really rare  these days to see a building where  concrete makes up the structure and the  cladding that's a massive thermal bridge  and it's probably a building a  scientists a worst nightmare  you have to insulate the outside of the  building to protect it from the cold to  keep the inside warm but the quality of  concrete is really interesting I think  concrete is almost like a natural  material but it's strong enough and  cheap enough to use for a large building  concrete is made with local aggregates  so the color and texture of the concrete  depends on the local climate and geology  so if you look closely every region it's  gonna have a different quality of  concrete concrete is also a very hard  material to work with and it's really  easy to screw up so anyone who's worked  with concrete knows that if we can get  it to be as beautiful as these brutalist  buildings then you deserve someone will  appraise actually one of the reasons I  decided to make the video was that I  found a reddit sub thread this was  actually in my first week quarantine  when I was kind of spiraling down the  black hole of Friday anyways I found a  subfloor as dedicated to Brutalism which  I found really interesting because it's  like one of the few architectural styles  that has its own reddit sub thread  especially interesting because my  experience of trying to talk to people  about architecture has never really been  that great I think one of the reasons  why Brutalism is becoming popular is  because they're so photogenic their  texture and their sharp  geometry and intense light conditions it  makes some perfect subject or backdrop  for a fashion photography and more than  ever before we consume architecture  through the screen rather than in person  so when we see the single shot of a  building that we never have to visit or  live in were suddenly drawn to their  radical quality one of the comments from  the reddit discussion was that they like  brutalist buildings because they're not  trying to be beautiful they kind of  stick out like a sore thumb in a sea of  agreeable buildings some would say it's  because they're so different because  they're so ugly because they don't give  - are they you know way kind of like  Bulldogs they're so ugly that they're  lovable do you think you could learn to  love these buildings so anyways that was  my brief introduction to Brutalism I  hope that was interesting for you guys  let me know what you think in the  comments below if you like this video  please like and subscribe and I'll see  you in the next video  you
 

